F shows that the reading of the removal step matters. Removing either operator alone leaves the system in I, so the test reports Supported, although removing both expels it. The cumulative reading reports Falsified. §10.2, step 3, can be read either way; the script uses the cumulative reading, which cannot miss a joint dependence on the external processes, and the two readings coincide in Table A1 because it has one external process. G and H show the opposite case. The SE is externally maintained and gates H and V, but redundancy lets the system survive without it, and both readings report Supported. This is the behavior §10.2, step 4, intends (a support whose removal does not expel the system is supporting). It also shows that the Falsified verdict of A comes from the absence of redundancy, not from the SE being external as such.
Scenario E assumes that M restores power whenever it is active. Table A4 relaxes this: on each step M restores power only with probability q, with the external supply cut and every external process removed. The load of 1.00 holds in all runs only at q = 1. It holds in 99.6% of runs at q = 0.95, in 74.9% at q = 0.80, in 38.1% at q = 0.70, and in at most 2% at q = 0.50 or below. The outcome is therefore a share of runs, not a single value, and a test of this kind would need replicates and a pre-registered reliability threshold for the regulator.
