Of the structures that depend on external processes, the cumulative reading flags all of them. This holds by construction, since its last step applies the same removal that defines the dependence. The one-at-a-time reading misses 2 to 5 per row, all of them structures with two external processes and redundant supports, the pattern of F. The cumulative reading also reports Falsified in 208 to 696 structures per row in which 𝓘 survives all external removal. In every one of them a declared constitutive component names an external process that it does not need. The test counts a component as externally maintained when it names an external process, and the script does not reclassify such a component downward. The rates in Table A5 depend on the generator and are not rates in any real system; what the table shows is that both kinds of error exist and which structures produce them.
Limits. The dependency structures are assumed, not measured. Health dynamics, thresholds, and the conjunctive form of “required regenerators” are modeling choices. The sensitivity analyses vary the numerical parameters (Table A2) and the dependency structure (Tables A3 to A5): redundancy, at-least-k requirements, two external processes, and an intermittent regulator. They do not vary the health dynamics, the requirement that dependencies be monotone (none is inhibitory, so a failure never reverses), the single resource used in the sweeps, or the stipulated regulator in scenario E. The model has no history register, no valuation ν, and no adaptive behavior; it tests only the closure logic of §3.1, §10.2 and §10.3.
